 "reviewHtml": "<div><p>If you need a gobsmacking party look in New York City, calling LaQuan Smith is a safe bet. The designer is fluent in sexed-up glam, corsets, cutouts, and celebrities. However, he’s trying to grow the remit.</p><p>“My woman has evolved. She’s not coming to me for the same things she used to ten seasons ago. She’s coming to me for a full 24 hour perspective,” said Smith. In other words, he’s doing night and day. It was only natural, then, that contradiction guided his spring collection: danger and delicateness, hard and soft, good and evil. One strength of the collection was Smith’s unlikely pairings—flirty, fluffy skirts with boyish, windbreaker jackets, for example.</p><p>Smith also took those thematic goalposts and went wild, quite literally. Embossed python forced the collars of a jacket upward and the peplum of a skirt outward, perky shoulder jackets were rendered in feathers and Bambi print. Another print merged those docile dots of a doe with the fierce pattern of a cheetah. (Smith also made a few pieces, including a sparkling gown, in collaboration with Paramount’s forthcoming movie Children of Blood and Bone.) The crowd was satisfied with the offer. Every phone, and a few gasps, went up as a breezy, barely-there orange gown floated down the runway.</p><p>As part of the effort to evolve with his customer, Smith has been introducing more tailored pieces into the mix—“bombshell suiting,” he called it. The best items fitting that bill were a couple of dark denim sets that could be zipped and unzipped at the wearer’s discretion; the denim lent them an air of approachability while the cut dressed them up. The LaQuan Smith spirit, tip-toeing into a new realm. However, models negotiating high heels, a slippy runway, and unhemmed pants or material pooling peskily around their ankles neither sustained the showtime fantasy nor helped sell the wearability message.</p><p>Still, there were bright spots and Smith has big ambitions: “I hope this really opens up a lot of eyes and perspectives for people to see I’m here to stay and that I can dress women all around the world,” he said.</p></div>",
